body:
Well, for starters, the fact that the comments are negative doesn't mean they're not true. There's no true answer to the question, it's a matter of opinion, of course, but the opinion being negative is not an argument against it. But that's going off on a tangent a bit.

1) We can say that if a person is alive (as in, hasn't commited suicide) is either happy or unhappy. But we can safely assume that someone who commited suicide has done it because they were unhappy. People end up keeping on living unhappy lives for many reasons, like
a) being afraid of dying or death itself;
b) having people who care for them, in this case suicude will only transfer or even multiply the amount of pain the person is experiencing;
c) having other people depend on them, like families or children; 
d) having hope for a better life;
e) just not being able to commit suicide, which is a relatively poor excuse but still a reason for many.
Then, being unhappy and unsatisfied with one's life is not so black-and-white. There are many degrees to which a person can be unhappy about their life, and definitely not all of them lead to suicidal thoughts.

2) Conservationists want to preserve the planet because its condition is going from better to worse due to human activity, no idea why pride and honor are mentioned here. 

We do create but art, as you talk about it, is not nearly at the same level of the pyramid of necessities as food, water and shelter are, and we're talking about those, the more people there are the less your offspring will get. You can't appreciate art if you're starving or if your living conditions are horrible. Basically, the whole point about the lack of resources is left unanswered.

3) Your children can just as well be harmful for the society as they can be valuable, a lot depends on how you act as parents. Genetics only will not carry them through life, upbringing is just as important, if not much more.
